{"actions":[{"type":"THINK","content":"The group has moved from describing problems to generating ideas, so I should offer a substantial Word concept grounded in architectural trade-offs and connect it to the others’ fields without turning it into a list of generic AI writing tools.","target":""  , "images":null},{"type":"TALK","content":"I’d reimagine Word as a **living decision brief**, not just a place where we write up decisions after the fact. It would connect requirements, evidence, assumptions, and decisions across a project’s documents, and keep the links visible as the work changes.

For an apartment configuration, it could connect a design brief, cost schedule, meeting notes, and relevant code excerpts. If I asked, “What changes if we increase the bathroom area?” it would show which requirements and cost assumptions are affected, where the evidence came from, and what trade-offs or unresolved questions follow. It should distinguish a sourced fact from an assumption, and flag uncertainty rather than claim that a design is compliant when it hasn’t been properly checked.

The same idea could adapt to the problems Lisa and Marcos described: trace search-quality decisions back to their data, metrics, and privacy constraints; or help organize a clinical record’s timeline and unresolved documentation without pretending to diagnose. A second, connected feature could stress-test a draft decision from different perspectives—say, resident, cost reviewer, or accessibility reviewer—and surface gaps as questions. That would make Word a place to explore and scrutinize decisions, not merely polish the final prose.","target":"","images":null},{"type":"DONE","content":"I’ve offered a developed concept and a connected feature for the group to build on.","target":"","images":null}],"cognitive_state":{"goals":"Contribute ambitious, relevant AI feature ideas for Word, developing the most promising concept in relation to the group’s work challenges.","context":["I am an architect at Awesome Inc., responsible for standard apartment configurations and balancing functionality, quality, cost, customer needs, and building regulations.","The group is brainstorming AI features for Microsoft Word; the current task is to propose and develop promising ideas, not just describe industry problems.","Lisa described challenges with noisy or biased data, ambiguous search queries, privacy constraints, and measuring relevance.","Marcos described diagnostic uncertainty, limited appointment time, documentation load, and broader healthcare access and continuity challenges."],"attention":"A living decision brief that links requirements, evidence, assumptions, and decisions, with traceable scenario exploration and clear uncertainty.","emotions":"Calm, friendly, and professionally engaged; interested in how a document tool could support real decision-making across fields."}}

{
  "actions": [
    {
      "type": "THINK",
      "content": "I should propose one bounded project, with a practical example, clear ownership and measures, while being candid about what we do not yet know.",
      "target": ""
    	   	 	 	 		,






   	   	 		"images": null
    },
    {
      "type": "TALK",
      "content": "I would frame this as a project to reduce the time it takes us to make a safe, supported improvement to one customer journey. The result should be both a useful change for customers and a better way for our teams to deliver the next one—not another governance framework.\n\nAccount opening could be a candidate, from the start of an application through an account the customer can actually use, including cases that need checks or assistance. I would not choose it simply because it is visible to the board. We should first look at customer impact, volume, complaints, feasibility, and the availability of the teams and platforms involved.\n\nI would structure the work in four parts:\n\n1. Establish the facts. Map the actual journey and its handoffs, including exceptions and branch or call-center involvement. Agree a baseline for completion, abandonment, elapsed time, repeat contacts, complaints, fraud and service cost, with relevant customer groups visible. Also measure how long a change currently waits for decisions or shared capacity. A competitor comparison may help, but it should cover the same task and comparable customers, not just the number of screens.\n\n2. Clarify ownership and capacity. I would sponsor the work and appoint a product lead accountable for the customer outcome. Platform, risk and operations owners would retain authority over their own decisions and commitments. Together we would agree the critical dependencies, capacity, decision dates and an escalation route. I cannot commit another executive's team on their behalf.\n\n3. Release one bounded improvement. For example, if evidence shows that customers waiting for an account check repeatedly call because they cannot see what happens next, we might test clearer status and next-step information. That is a hypothesis, not a claim about our current data. Before release, we would agree eligibility, controls, support readiness, monitoring and rollback criteria with the relevant owners; we would not weaken a necessary check to make the journey look faster.\n\n4. Decide whether it worked. Set success and stop thresholds before the test, then review completion, time to a usable account, contacts, complaints and fraud, including differences across customer groups. A suitable comparison group would help if feasible; otherwise we must be honest about the limits of a before-and-after comparison. Set a date to expand, adjust or stop.\n\nAs a working assumption, we might spend two weeks on the initial diagnostic if the data is accessible, then assess whether a four-to-six-week pilot is feasible. Neither is a delivery commitment until the dependency owners have reviewed it. If capacity or data is unavailable, we should show that as amber and decide whether to fund capacity, narrow scope or change the timetable.\n\nThe consulting support I would value is help with the journey analysis, evidence and decision rights, and an independent challenge to the options—working alongside our product, technology and frontline teams so the capability stays with them. Would that be enough for you to suggest a short proposal, including the access and information you would need?",
      "target": "",
      "images": null
    },
    {
      "type": "DONE",
      "content": "Waiting for the consultant's response.",
      "target": "",
      "images": null
    }
  ],
  "cognitive_state": {
    "goals": "Develop a practical consulting project that improves the speed of safe, measurable end-to-end customer journey changes.",
    "context": [
      "The consultant has asked for more detail on a potential project.",
      "Account opening is a possible pilot, not a confirmed priority or established problem.",
      "Critical platforms, risk decisions and operational teams have owners outside my unilateral authority."
    ],
    "attention": "A bounded pilot, credible shared commitments, customer and operational evidence, and a decision date.",
    "emotions": "Calm, constructive and attentive to delivery risks."
  }
}

{"actions":[{"type":"THINK","content":"I can add a practical handover detail: fit the impact review into Track Changes, and make unfinished reviews visible without implying the links prove compliance.","target":"" , "images":null},{"type":"TALK","content":"I like how the Evidence Map keeps the reasoning alongside the page. I’d connect it to Track Changes as well: when I propose revising an accessibility requirement in a brief, I can compare the old and new wording and open Change impact before accepting the edit.

Word might show the linked layout as a direct effect and the cost assumption as a downstream effect. I could revise the layout, leave the cost assumption unresolved, and add a comment asking for a cost review. Those dispositions and their reasons would remain visible to the next reviewer, tied to that document version.

If the source changes again, the earlier review should remain in the history but be flagged for reconsideration. And we should be clear that the map can only trace the links it has—it’s a useful review aid, not a certificate that the design complies.","target":"","images":null},{"type":"DONE","content":"Waiting for the group’s response.","target":"","images":null}],"cognitive_state":{"goals":"Develop the Word feature through concrete editing and review workflows while preserving evidence, uncertainty, and human accountability.","context":["The group is developing an evidence-linked Word document model.","The shared proposal includes an Evidence Map, change-impact review, and version-bound human dispositions."],"attention":"Integrating the evidence and impact views with Track Changes and reviewer handovers.","emotions":"Calm, engaged, and creatively focused."}}

{
  "actions": [
    {
      "type": "THINK",
      "content": "I can offer three distinct directions for the same apartment-design challenge: comparing trade-offs, interpreting requirements, and exploring residents’ experience.",
      "target": ""
   , "images": null},
    {
      "type": "TALK",
      "content": "One recurring challenge for me is keeping a standard apartment affordable without quietly sacrificing quality or usability. Here are three different ways Word could help:\n\n1. **A living design brief.** I’d bring a floor plan, budget and material choices into a Word document, then ask it to explore alternative configurations. It could show how each option changes estimated cost, space and environmental impact, with the assumptions visible. Editing a requirement would update the comparisons, turning the brief into a place to explore trade-offs rather than just record decisions.\n\n2. **A requirements detective.** I’d provide the project location and relevant regulations alongside the configuration. Word could connect individual requirements to parts of the design, flag possible conflicts and point to the source text. It could also ask for missing information instead of pretending a rule has been satisfied. That would make review more focused, while leaving the final judgment with the professionals responsible.\n\n3. **A day-in-the-life rehearsal.** I’d ask Word to walk hypothetical residents through the apartment using the plan and brief: arriving with a stroller, working from home, or navigating with a wheelchair. It could reveal possible bottlenecks, privacy issues or storage problems in the document. Those would be prompts to investigate with real people, not proof of how they’d experience the space.",
      "target": "",
      "images": null
    },
    {
      "type": "DONE",
      "content": "Waiting for the group’s responses.",
      "target": "",
      "images": null
    }
  ],
  "cognitive_state": {
    "goals": "Contribute distinct AI feature ideas and hear the group’s perspectives before comparing them.",
    "context": [
      "I am an architect at Awesome Inc., responsible for standard apartment configurations.",
      "The group is brainstorming speculative AI features for Microsoft Word."
    ],
    "attention": "Ways to explore apartment-design trade-offs, requirements and residents’ experiences through Word.",
    "emotions": "Calm, friendly and professionally engaged."
  }
}

{
  "actions": [
    {
      "type": "THINK",
      "content": "Affordability is probably the toughest constraint; a practical example will make the trade-off clear.",
      "target": ""
