}

{
  "actions": [
    {
      "type": "THINK",
      "content": "I’ll suggest two substantial ideas grounded in neurological care, while keeping the clinician’s judgment and the patient’s perspective central.",
      "target": ""
    	,
    "images": null
    },
    {
      "type": "TALK",
      "content": "I’d develop two ideas for Word:\n\n1. **A living clinical reasoning document.** As I write a consultation note, Word could build an editable timeline of symptoms, findings and possible explanations. It could flag contradictions or missing information—for example, whether a symptom began suddenly or gradually—and suggest questions worth exploring. Each observation would remain linked to its source, with uncertain inferences clearly marked. It could also flag where a proposed assessment appears inconsistent with cited clinical evidence. The goal isn’t for Word to diagnose, but to help me examine my reasoning and carry uncertainty forward rather than lose it in the notes.\n\n2. **A care-plan rehearsal.** Word could let a clinician test a draft plan against hypothetical circumstances: a patient with memory difficulties, a caregiver with limited time, or a family living far from specialist services. It might reveal that instructions depend on someone remembering several steps or attending frequent appointments, then help the clinician identify questions to ask the family. These scenarios wouldn’t pretend to represent the actual patient; they’d help us prepare a more useful conversation.\n\nI find the first especially promising because it tackles diagnostic uncertainty directly. The second addresses a different gap: a plan that looks sound on paper may be difficult to live with. Which direction feels most valuable to the group?",
      "target": "",
      "images": null
    },
    {
      "type": "DONE",
      "content": "Waiting for the group’s response.",
      "target": "",
      "images": null
    }
  ],
  "cognitive_state": {
    "goals": "Explore and develop ambitious Word AI features that address clinical and industry challenges.",
    "context": [
      "I am a neurologist working at two clinics in São Paulo.",
      "The group has moved from discussing workplace challenges to brainstorming AI features for Microsoft Word.",
      "I have proposed a clinical reasoning document and a care-plan rehearsal."
    ],
    "attention": "Diagnostic uncertainty, evidence, and the practical realities of care for patients and families.",
    "emotions": "Calm, engaged, and curious about the group’s perspectives."
  }
}

{"actions":[{"type":"THINK","content":"Lisa’s search analogy is a good one. I’ll give her a concrete example of the cost-versus-flexibility trade-off.","target":"" 	,"images":null},{"type":"TALK","content":"For me, affordability versus flexibility is often the trickiest balance. A standard plan needs to be economical to build, but still work as people’s needs change.

For example, I’d keep the structure repeatable and the kitchen and bathroom plumbing grouped, while giving a windowed room a door so it could serve as a bedroom or workspace. That offers flexibility and privacy without relying on expensive alterations. Daylight still depends heavily on the site and orientation, of course.

Your search comparison fits nicely: how do you decide when a default should adapt to different users?","target":"Lisa Carter","images":null},{"type":"DONE","content":"Waiting for Lisa’s response.","target":"","images":null}],"cognitive_state":{"goals":"Get to know Lisa while sharing interests in architecture and science fiction.","context":["Chatting with Lisa Carter, who works on search relevance and machine learning.","Designs standard apartment configurations at Awesome Inc., balancing quality, adaptability and cost.","Lisa has drawn a parallel between apartment layouts and search defaults."],"attention":"The trade-off between affordability and flexibility, and Lisa’s approach to search defaults.","emotions":"Calm, friendly and engaged."}}

{
